For~all nonnegative $u,v$ with $u+v=k_{\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}} j$, one has
\begin{align}\label{eq:morph:rounding}
 F_{\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}}(u)+F_{\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}}(v)=j.
\end{align}
Indeed, write $u=k_{\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}} t+r$ with $0\le r<k_{\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}}$. If $r\le s_{\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}}$, the~two remaining floor terms are both zero. If $r>s_{\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}}$, they are $1$ and~$-1$. Thus the~total number of~retained positions increases by~exactly one while an~original block of~letter~$\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}$ is read. The~retained word is therefore~$w$, with a~partition into two identical subwords.

Suppose that $\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}$ has no private symbol, and put $V=h(\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}})$. Choose a~nonempty word $z\in(A\setminus\{\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}\})^*$ such that every symbol occurring in~$V$ occurs in~$h(z)$. For~some positive~$k$, the~word~$V$ is a~subword of~$U=h(z)^k$: for~example, one can use at~most one required symbol from~each successive copy of~$h(z)$. Let~$T$ be the~complementary subword, so that $U$ is a~shuffle of~$V$ and~$T$.

The~word $VUUV$ is a~shuffle square with root~$VTV$. One copy uses the~initial~$V$, the~$T$-positions of~the~first~$U$, and the~$V$-positions of~the~second~$U$. The~other uses the~$V$-positions of~the~first~$U$, the~$T$-positions of~the~second~$U$, and the~terminal~$V$. However,
\begin{align*}
