\end{align*}
and $\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}z^{2k}\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}$ is not a~shuffle square. Its only two occurrences of~$\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}$ are at~its ends. A~root would contain exactly one~$\textcolor{purple!69!black}{\mathtt{a}}$, which would have to~be both its first and its last letter, although the~root has length greater than~one. This is a~contradiction. Private symbols of~different letters are distinct, giving the~cardinality assertion.

